<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tongwen Guan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1862">1862</a>–<a href="/years/1902">1902</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as School of Combined Learning</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tongwen Guan, or School of Combined Learning, was a government school of Western languages and science founded at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> in 1862 to train interpreters for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zongli-yamen">Zongli Yamen</a>. It opened with ten students of English; a proposal to add astronomy and mathematics was opposed by conservatives led by Woren, who feared &quot;learning from foreigners&quot;. By 1877, with W. A. P. Martin as dean, it taught foreign languages and subjects from chemistry to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-law">international law</a> to over a hundred students. In 1902 it was absorbed into the Imperial University of Peking, now Peking University.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tongwen-guan.jpg" alt="Tongwen Guan" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:W._A._P._Martin_&amp;_faculty.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
